# .gitignore — Android Pentesting Notes
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE THIS FILE EXISTS TO ENFORCE: SOURCES, NEVER BINARIES.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This repository ships the SOURCE of every lab target, fixture and example,
# plus the script that builds it. It never ships the built binary. That is an
# editorial policy, not an oversight, and it is worth stating because the
# alternative is so much more convenient:
#
#   1. A committed .apk is an unsigned-by-you executable of unknown provenance
#      that a reader installs on a device with developer options enabled. The
#      "deliberately vulnerable app" category has been abused exactly this way.
#      Sources plus a build script let the reader read what they are about to
#      run. See 73-Labs/README.md section 5.2.
#
#   2. A committed binary rots. The APK built against build-tools 34 behaves
#      differently from the one a reader builds today, and the chapter's
#      measured claims are pinned to the build, not to the source.
#
#   3. A signing key in version control is worse than an APK. Every build
#      script here generates a throwaway key at build time, into its output
#      directory, outside the tree.
#
# Because of this, several chapters' build scripts deliberately build OUT OF
# TREE — 43-WebView/examples/build.sh is the reference implementation, and its
# closing line is the convention in one sentence: "APKs are under $OUTDIR --
# nothing was written into the repository." Those scripts default to $TMPDIR
# and need no help from this file.
#
# The rules below exist for the scripts that default to an IN-TREE ./out/, and
# as a backstop for everyone else. The repository's own verification script
# treats the extensions in the first block as a hard blocklist: a match is a
# build failure, not a warning.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO THINGS THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES *NOT* IGNORE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   *.log      Stock Android and Gradle .gitignore templates all carry `*.log`.
#              This one must not. 22-SELinux ships two .log files that are
#              MEASURED EVIDENCE, not build noise:
#                  22-SELinux/examples/avc-denials-measured.log
#                  22-SELinux/labs/ctf-quiet-domain/avc.log
#              Adding `*.log` here would silently delete a chapter's proof.
#              If you need to ignore a log, name it.
#
#   .gitkeep   406 of them hold open the empty examples/, images/, labs/,
#              references/ and scripts/ directories that every chapter is
#              required to have. They are content. The negation at the bottom
#              of this file is a belt-and-braces guard, since git cannot
#              re-include a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY.
#
# ===========================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The artefact blocklist — enforced, not advisory
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled or packaged output of any kind. If one of these appears in a diff,
# something built into the tree that should have built out of it.

# Python
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
__pycache__/

# Run scripts with `python3 -B` so __pycache__ is never created in the first
# place. Every script header in this repository says so.

# JVM
*.class
*.jar
*.war
*.ear

# Android packages and bytecode
*.apk
*.apks
*.aab
*.dex
*.odex
*.vdex
*.art
*.aar
*.ap_

# Native
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.so.*
*.dylib
*.dll
*.elf
*.obj


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Key material — never, under any circumstance
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build scripts generate a throwaway signing key into their output directory at
# build time. Nothing in this class is ever committed, not even a debug key.

*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pfx
*.bks
*.pem
*.key
debug.ks
keystore.properties


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. In-tree build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Most build scripts write to $TMPDIR. These are the ones that default to a
# directory beside their sources — 15-Native-Code, 18-ARM, 19-Memory,
# 23-Permissions, 26-Intent and 52-StaticAnalysis all use ./out/. Two chapters
# already carry their own local .gitignore saying exactly this; these rules
# cover the rest.

out/
out-*/
build/
bin/
gen/
obj/
dist/

# Chapter script output with distinctive names
pulled-apks/
aosp-recon-*/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Gradle and the Android build system
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No Gradle project is committed here — the examples build with bare
# build-tools precisely so a reader needs no Gradle and no network. These are a
# backstop for anyone who opens an example in Android Studio.

.gradle/
gradle-app.setting
gradlew
gradlew.bat
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
local.properties
.externalNativeBuild/
.cxx/
captures/
lint/intermediates/
lint/generated/
lint/outputs/
lint/tmp/
proguard/
release/

# NOTE: `.gradle/` has a trailing slash so it matches only the Gradle cache
# DIRECTORY. The `build.gradle` and `*.gradle` FILES that chapters ship as
# reading material are unaffected, as are the `*.pro` ProGuard/R8 rule files in
# 41-ProGuard and 42-R8, which are chapter content.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. IDE and editor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.idea/
.project
.classpath
.settings/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.\#*
\#*\#


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Operating system noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Git Bash / MSYS2 drops these beside the working directory when a native tool
# crashes. Observed here: a `grep.exe.stackdump` appeared at the repository root
# during a verification sweep. Harmless, but it is an artefact and it recurs.
*.stackdump
core
core.[0-9]*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Analysis working directories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decompiler and unpacker output. These are large, machine-generated, and in
# several cases contain third-party code pulled from a device under test —
# which must never enter this repository for licensing reasons as well as
# artefact ones.

*_jadx/
*_apktool/
jadx-out/
apktool-out/
unpacked/
decompiled/
*.dump
*.hprof
frida-server*
*.frida-session


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Maintainer's build log — deliberately not published
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BUILD-STATUS.md is the internal engineering record kept during construction:
# the measured findings, the device table, and a candid log of every defect
# found in the chapters AND in the checking scripts themselves.
#
# It is excluded because it is build scaffolding rather than content, and it is
# written in the vocabulary of the process that produced it rather than for a
# reader. Excluding it is a publication decision, not a deletion — the file
# stays in the working tree.
#
# Every chapter that used to cite it has been rewritten to state the underlying
# fact inline, so nothing a reader needs depends on a file they cannot see. If
# you re-introduce a reference to it, inline the fact instead.
BUILD-STATUS.md

# planning-guide.md — editorial-pass working notes, kept unpublished for the same reason
planning-guide.md

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Guard: .gitkeep is content
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept last so it cannot be overridden by a later rule.

!.gitkeep
